INSERT INTO public.blog_posts (slug,title,description,body,category,reading_time,author_name,author_role,faqs,cta_label,cta_href,status,published_at) VALUES (
$tavara$what-the-tavara-nurse-actually-cooks-trinidad$tavara$,
$tavara$What the Tavara Nurse Actually Cooks (and What She Doesn't)$tavara$,
$tavara$Meals are where most in-home care arrangements quietly break down. Here is exactly what is included at the Tavara baseline, what is not, and how to ask for more without breeding resentment.$tavara$,
$tavara$There is a household in Arima where, for the first three weeks, everything was fine. The nurse cooked every other day for the mother she was caring for. Pot of stew chicken, some peas, ground provisions, enough to stretch.

Then the daughter who lives there started eating the food too. Not much, a plate at lunch, a small dish in the evening. Reasonable, by family logic.

By week six the nurse was cooking almost daily. The food was finishing too quickly. She had stopped batch-cooking because there was nothing left to freeze. She was tired and she was getting quietly bitter, the kind of bitter that does not show up on a daily log but shows up in how she answers the phone.

The daughter had no idea. From her seat, the nurse was just cooking, and she was just eating what was in the pot.

That is the meal conversation. It is the most common reason an arrangement that started well starts to wobble around the two-month mark. So let us name what is actually included, what is not, and how to ask for more without anyone resenting anyone.

## The baseline: what is actually included

At the standard Tavara care rate, the nurse handles light cooking. Specifically:

- **Pre-prepared, already-seasoned (or lightly seasoned) meats.** The family supplies the protein cleaned, cut, and seasoned, or close to it. The nurse cooks it.
- **Batch cooking of peas and dried beans.** Pressure cook a pot of pigeon peas or red beans, portion, freeze. Pull out as needed.
- **Cooking typically happens every other day, not daily.** The rhythm is cook, eat, eat the leftovers warm, then cook again. This is how most working T&T households actually run.
- **Breakfast the way your household actually eats it.** If the loved one has bread and peanut butter most mornings, the nurse will not suddenly start frying bakes. If she takes bush tea and a soft-boiled egg, that is what the nurse prepares. We capture this at onboarding so the nurse walks in already knowing.
- **Plating, warming, and feeding if needed.** Including patience with someone who eats slowly, or someone whose appetite shifts day to day.

That is the baseline. It covers most households, most of the time.

## Who the food is for

The loved one receiving care. Always.

Sometimes the spouse, when the nurse agrees and the household dynamic makes sense. In many T&T homes, one spouse has been cooking and serving the other for forty years. When that spouse is the one now needing care, the nurse stepping into that role often includes making a plate for the partner too. That is a conversation between the nurse, the family, and the coordinator, not an assumption.

Never for grandchildren in the home. Never for the adult son who pops in for lunch. Never for the cousin who is staying for a week. Never for Sunday family lunch.

This line is not us being mean. It is the line that protects the nurse from absorbing the meal load of a six-person household on a one-person budget, which is what happens in the Arima story above and what we will not let happen on our watch.

> The baseline is one person fed properly, not a whole household fed casually.

## What the nurse will not do

To save you a difficult phone call later, here is what the standard rate does not cover:

- **Cleaning a whole chicken from scratch.** Gutting, parsing, deboning, seasoning, bagging, freezing. That is prep. Prep is a separate skill and a separate ask.
- **Daily from-scratch cooking for special diets** unless this has been written into a plan with a Meal Support upgrade and the nurse has agreed to it.
- **Grocery shopping for the household.** A short top-up run for one or two items the kitchen ran out of, sometimes, with the family's money and prior agreement. A full weekly shop, no.
- **Cooking for catered events, birthdays you are hosting, or the wake.** She can serve and clean up around feeding the loved one. She is not the caterer.

This is not a complete list. It is the four asks that come up most often and where families are most surprised.

## Holidays, birthdays, and the things she may do anyway

Christmas, Old Year's Night, Divali, Eid, Easter, Mother's Day, the loved one's birthday. National foods. Pastelles. Sweet bread. Sorrel.

If the nurse has formed a real bond with the household, and she has the skill, she may quietly cook something extra on those days. Some nurses love that part of the work. Some do not, and that is also fine.

What we ask families to do: never assume it. If you would like something extra for Mother's Day, ask the coordinator a week in advance. Sometimes the answer will be yes with a small extra arrangement. Sometimes it will be no and we will help you find another way. Both answers are okay. The not-okay version is pressuring the nurse on the day.

## When you need more than the baseline

There are real reasons to need more cooking than the baseline:

- A nutritionist has written a meal plan and the loved one needs it followed precisely.
- A doctor has recommended a renal, diabetic, or cardiac diet with specific limits.
- The loved one is in recovery and needs five or six small meals a day instead of two or three.
- The household culture is high-effort cooking and going to the baseline feels like a downgrade.

Two paths to handle this properly:

1. **Add the Meal Support upgrade at onboarding.** This is a billable add-on through your Care Coordinator. It is named, scoped, agreed to by the nurse, and priced into the arrangement so no one is doing extra work for free.
2. **Move up a subscription tier.** Active Care and Premium tiers match you with nurses who are not just willing but trained and equipped for structured cooking. The coordinator will walk you through which tier fits your household.

If you are not sure which one is right, ask. We would much rather front-load that conversation than fix a resentful kitchen in month three.

## How JAPP fits

A quick word for families weighing options. The Geriatric Adolescent Partnership Programme (GAPP) and similar public-sector supports in T&T do important work and meet the bare minimum for families who qualify. They are a real resource. We refer families to them when that is the right fit.

What GAPP and similar programmes are not built for is the personalised side of the meal question. They will not sit with you at onboarding to learn that your mother only drinks tea from the white cup, or that your father will not eat anything green unless it is hidden in the rice. That observation, the watching of how your household actually eats, is what Tavara nurses do as a matter of course.

If your needs are simple and the public option fits, use it. If the meal piece is where the care actually lives in your house, Tavara is built for that.

## Why we name all of this at onboarding

The Arima household is fixed now. The nurse is cooking every other day again. The daughter contributes for her own meals or eats out. Nobody is bitter. We caught it because the nurse mentioned it on a check-in and we had the conversation with the family the next day.

But we much prefer the version where the conversation happens before the first pot of food. So at onboarding we walk through:

- Who is being cooked for, by first name.
- What the loved one eats for breakfast, lunch, dinner, normally.
- Who supplies and preps the protein, and how often.
- What happens on holidays and birthdays.
- What happens if a second household member starts eating the food.

It takes twenty minutes. It saves months.

If you are arranging care now and you have not had this conversation, raise it. Either with your coordinator or in your first sit-down with the nurse. The cost of asking is small. The cost of not asking is the Arima household.$tavara$,
