$tavara$[{"q":"Can the nurse cook for my husband too?","a":"Often yes, if the household dynamic makes sense (your mother used to cook for your father, for example) and the nurse agrees at onboarding. It is a named conversation between you, the nurse, and the coordinator, not an assumption. We write it into the care plan so nobody is guessing."},{"q":"What if my mother is on a renal diet?","a":"That sits outside the baseline. You have two options. Add the Meal Support upgrade through your coordinator, which prices the extra effort into the arrangement, or step up to a subscription tier that matches you with a nurse trained for structured dietary cooking. Either way, the meal plan gets written down and the nurse gets time to follow it properly."},{"q":"We have a big family lunch on Sundays. Can the nurse help?","a":"She can plate, warm, and feed your loved one during the lunch. She is not the cook for the family meal. If you want help with the family meal itself, that is a catering or domestic help conversation, not a care coordination one, and we are happy to point you in the right direction."},{"q":"Why every other day and not daily?","a":"Because cooking daily is how a nurse burns out by month three. Batch cooking, freezing, and warming is how most working T&T households actually feed themselves. It is sustainable, it preserves the food, and it leaves the nurse the energy to be present for the rest of the care, which is the part you are really paying for."}]$tavara$::jsonb,
